{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-04T11:51:04.775Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"28","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed on the PR branch (baf916c7). CONFIRMED. CHOICE: decline the selector on Windows, keep POSIX quoting on POSIX. The reasoning matters: to cmd.exe a single quote is an ORDINARY character, so the emitted \"'a\u0026b'\" still runs 'b' — the quoting was a false guarantee about exactly the characters it was added to neutralise. PowerShell meanwhile reads \"'\" as a quote and mis-parses the bare form cmd.exe needs, so the two Windows shells disagree with each other AND with POSIX, and no single string is correct. On Windows the selector is therefore withheld (the definition line still shows file and line); bare-word values keep their unquoted selector everywhere. The guard TestDisambiguationSelectorsQuoteRepositoryControlledValues keeps every assertion and now calls disambiguationSelectorsFor(\"linux\", ...) so it is host-independent, following the repo's existing linkTargetAnchorFor(goos, ...) pattern. Fail-without-fix: 'definition 0 advertised --symbol State --file 'src/a b.go' --line 12 on Windows'.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NPR3CK3QATMJVRDA4A2WS9","targetId":"01M1NPR3CTDSZ3E3YJHVPB64J3","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NF973R4BZXAJK78QWWZ364"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
